WEBVTT

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.347
Hi, friends. Gani here. This is The Room Reader.

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:20.693
Sit tall in your chair. Feet flat on the floor.

00:00:20.693 --> 00:00:24.501
Hands resting on your desk. Shoulders soft.

00:00:24.501 --> 00:00:28.309
Take one slow breath with me… and let it go.

00:00:28.309 --> 00:00:37.194
Every room has weather. Not rain weather — people weather. Some mornings the room is bright and busy. Some afternoons it is slow and sleepy.

00:00:37.194 --> 00:00:47.349
The weather is made of little signals. How loud the voices are. How fast the feet move. The look on the faces around you. The sound of your teacher's voice.

00:00:47.349 --> 00:00:58.773
And here is the first thing to know about weather: it is nobody's fault. A loud room is not a bad room. Weather just is.

00:00:58.773 --> 00:01:03.850
You can learn to read it — the way you learn to read the sky before you go outside. Let me show you.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:15.274
Start with your ears. Is the room humming loud, or soft? Fast voices, or slow ones? Your ears take the room's temperature.

00:01:15.274 --> 00:01:26.697
Then your eyes. Look at the faces — not to judge them, just to notice. Relaxed faces, busy faces, a face that looks a little stormy today.

00:01:26.697 --> 00:01:38.121
If you read a stormy face, here is the whole rule: be gentle, and leave it be. A stormy face is not a problem for you to fix, and it is never a story to tell.

00:01:38.121 --> 00:01:48.276
Reading is noticing so you can care. It is never spying. It is never policing. And it is never reporting on anyone. What you notice stays with you.

00:01:48.276 --> 00:01:49.545
You never name what you read — not out loud, not later. The reading is quiet, and it is kind, and it is yours.

00:01:49.545 --> 00:02:00.969
Once you have read the weather, there is one dial you can always turn: your own.

00:02:00.969 --> 00:02:12.393
A loud, wiggly room might call for the opposite from you — a softer voice, gentler steps, a little more space around your body. Or a sleepy room might call for your calm, steady energy.

00:02:12.393 --> 00:02:21.278
You cannot change the whole room, and that is not your job. But your own setting is always yours to change — even when the room stays exactly the same.

00:02:21.278 --> 00:02:22.547
That is what a room reader does. Reads the weather. Sets their own dial. Carries on kindly.

00:02:22.547 --> 00:02:31.432
Read the weather. Set your dial. Be gentle.

00:02:31.432 --> 00:02:37.779
Shoulders soft. Let your body rest.

00:02:37.779 --> 00:02:42.856
Hands down. One more slow breath… and let it go.

00:02:42.856 --> 00:02:49.203
Eyes open. Look around your classroom.

00:02:49.203 --> 00:02:55.549
You're ready for what's next.
